  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/a/a3/Fantastic_Four_Vol_1_26.png",
  "plot_summary": "[RECAP: The Hulk has come to New York City to take vengeance on the Avengers because of their decision to replace him in their ranks with Captain America.&nbsp; With the rest of the Fantastic Four sidelined, the Thing was left to face him all by himself... and eventually lost.&nbsp; However, even as the Hulk lumbers away, brave Ben Grimm resolutely returns to the battle, though the odds seem stacked against him...]\n\nThe Thing hurls himself into renewed combat with the Hulk, who thought he was finally done with him.&nbsp; The onlooking army guys admire the Thing's courage and point out that \"good thing they're battling in a condemned neighborhood!&nbsp; They're saving the wreckers a lot of work!\"\n\nMeanwhile, we check in on Reed, who desperately wants to help Ben but can't due to the raging fever inside him, and Sue, who won't leave his side.&nbsp; The Human Torch wakes up in the hospital and manages to fly out to help Ben, but all he does is distract and anger him!&nbsp; The Hulk claims he's \"been betrayed by everyone I've ever known -- but no more!&nbsp; From now on, the <i>Hulk </i>strikes first!\"&nbsp; Soon, the Torch is too weak to continue, and Ben turns back to the battle, quipping that the \"business of bein' the idol of millions gets tougher all the time!\"\n\nBut the Hulk seems to have suddenly vanished.&nbsp; We find him below, hijacking a subway.&nbsp; He even gives the driver time to evacuate the passengers!&nbsp; He then drives the subway and continues on his way toward the Avengers.&nbsp; Once he reaches the \"expensive section of New York's upper east side,\" he emerges from a manhole and approaches \"Anthony Stark's mansion.\"\n\nHe walks right in on the Avengers, this time with Captain America.&nbsp; Rick Jones is also present and wants to reason with him.&nbsp; But the Hulk is looking <i>specifically</i> for Rick, who he feels left him for the Avengers!&nbsp; The close quarters hamper the Avengers, which allows the Hulk to grab Rick and crash out through a wall.&nbsp; Only the Wasp has the vantage point to see where he left and follow!\n\nThankfully, in a city hospital across town, an antidote has been found for the illness that has plagued Reed Richards, and he immediately suits up (against his doctor's orders to recuperate).&nbsp; Johnny has now had time to rest a bit as well, so the FF are all back together at last!&nbsp; They overhear the army guys saying that word has arrived that the Avengers are pursuing the Hulk!&nbsp; So the FF jump into the Fantasti-Car and set out in search of the battle.\n\nThe FF locate the Hulk, whose ear the Wasp is buzzing around in, trying to annoy him enough to give Rick Jones a chance to escape.&nbsp; Yet it seems that the FF have arrived at the exact same time as the Avengers!&nbsp; They begin to get in each other's way, even to the point where the Thing accidentally steps into the path of Thor's hammer (the one thing Hulk fears, according to the text).&nbsp; When Ben sizes up Thor, he can't resist asking \"Say, Curly -- is that getup of yours for <i>real</i>?\" They finally agree that working together, they might be able to stop the Hulk, to which Ben says to Thor \"Say, for a guy with long hair, you're A-okay!&nbsp; You've even got muscles in your <i>voice</i>!\"\n\nThe Hulk has been spotted by the police atop a new construction site, where the Human Torch finds him confronting Rick Jones, angry about his apparent team-up with Captain America and the Avengers, even while Rick continues to argue that the Hulk has misread the situation.&nbsp; The Torch attacks, but Iron Man hurls him aside with his repulsors, claiming that Johnny is just wasting his flame and that it will take more than just heat to take down the Hulk.&nbsp; This actually snuffs out Johnny's fire, requiring Reed to step forward and rescue the now-plummeting Johnny.&nbsp; Meanwhile, the Hulk pins Iron Man under some steel girders, requiring a rescue by Thor.\n\nIn the midst of the battle, the Thing has had time to climb the girders and get near the Hulk.&nbsp; He declares to the Hulk that <i>this </i>time, he's \"takin' off the kid gloves!\"&nbsp; But the force of their punches knocks the Thing into a vat of wet cement.&nbsp; Captain America and then Giant-Man have a go at the Hulk before the Wasp brings a cavalry of ants, which cause the Hulk to itch all over, giving Rick Jones time to hurl a \"gamma-ray treated capsule which Banner gave me months ago\" into his mouth!&nbsp; The Hulk heads toward the Hudson River to try to get the ants off of him.&nbsp; He disappears into its depths and seemingly vanishes, long enough for the FF and the Avengers to think the battle is over, but the reader is shown the face of Dr. Banner, who surfaces \"a few minutes later.\"\n\nIn the aftermath, Rick points out that he knew he was never really in any danger, and that his \"association\" with Captain America was really more about the fact that he reminded Cap of Bucky.&nbsp; Both teams acknowledge that it was good to have met the other.\n\nThe FF walk away, happy that it's all over.",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "Other than the splash page recap, the issue opens almost as if you'd just turned the page after last month's tale ended.&nbsp; The stirring image of the Thing pulling himself up off the ground and staggering once more into battle with the Hulk seems to now be incidental, and it's as if there hasn't been an entire month of waiting for the readers -- the battle simply resumes...\n\n[ASSET: left | StayPutKid | width=14%] At least this time, the co-creators pause to show what's happening to the other three team members, even suggesting that Reed might actually recover quite <i>soon</i>, and even having Johnny return to the battle and offer aid to his teammate, feeble as it may have been.&nbsp; The Thing quite reasonably tells him to back off and leave the Hulk to him.\n\nThe action is interrupted here and there with brief scenes involving the army, either:\n[HANG] moving into position to help out (in whatever way possible)\n[HANG] actually firing missiles at the Hulk (albeit futilely)\n[HANG] OR news reporters butting into the action to interview the Thing (who has better things to do)\n\n[ASSET: right | HijackedTrain | width=17%] All of that serves to burn through almost 8 pages of story, leading to the [SPAN: severity=error; text=worst sequence in the book that makes no sense at all.]&nbsp; It features the Hulk <i>hijacking</i> a subway train, giving its passengers time to get out, and then driving it toward \"Anthony Stark's mansion\" to find the Avengers.\n[BR]\n[BR] This is the same Hulk who demanded a lift from a couple of truck drivers last month.\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=error; text=And I'm <i>sorry</i>, but this is the same Hulk that is acting <i>entirely</i> out of character!]\n\nIn what <i>universe</i> does the Hulk need a train to make his way to the Avengers?&nbsp; (And don't say that he was simply trying to avoid the Thing's interference -- he'd already demonstrated that the Thing was nothing more than a nuisance.)\n\n[DIVIDER]\nAs the book was approaching its midway point, it was time to bring in the guest stars.\n[BR] <b><i>Presto!</i></b>  The Hulk bursts in on the Avengers.\n[ASSET: center | GrabbingRick | width=large]\n\nExcept that this month, he seems to have forgotten about being mad about them replacing him with Captain America.&nbsp; No, <i>this</i> month he seems focused only on Rick Jones.&nbsp; <b>Destroying</b> the Avengers seems to have become only secondary now.\n\n\n<b>FIXME</b>\n\n[ASSET: left | FearTheHammer | width=12%] <b>FIXME</b>\n\n[ASSET: center | TeamsMeetAtLast | width=large]\n<b>FIXME</b>\n\n[ASSET: right | RollCameras | width=12%] <b>FIXME</b>\n\n[ASSET: left | GammaRayCapsule | width=12%] <b>FIXME</b>\n\n[ASSET: center | HappyEnding | width=large]\n<b>FIXME</b>\n\n\nA mostly by-the-numbers plot, with the team-up of the Avengers and the FF being the major selling point of the book, but without any genuine payoff of lasting consequence.&nbsp; I thought the last issue was better, even with its questionable \"Reed gets sick\" and \"Torch gets hurt\" aspects to prevent them from getting involved, leaving us with the Thing/Hulk battle which really was the showcase of that issue.&nbsp; The fact of the matter is that the whole sequence probably lasted no more than an hour or two, but during that time, Reed is miraculously cured, and the Torch also finds the strength to fly back into battle.\n\nThe two teams getting in each other's way was fine -- <i>expected</i> even -- as there were a lot of people going at the Hulk at the same time.&nbsp; But as the number of pages left began to dwindle, Kirby was forced to wind things down with the introduction of the ants to drive him away.&nbsp; The fact that no one even <i>tried</i> to locate him after that is suspect and affected this issue's final rating (not to mention that miraculous \"gamma pill\" that Rick throws into the Hulk's mouth -- <i>really</i>?).&nbsp; I'm not sure where the Hulk will show up next, but perhaps at least Rick had time enough to get it into his head that he didn't betray him -- we can only hope!\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nI won't lie:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; text=this follow-up to last month's strong performer was mostly disappointing.]\n\nI admit that the \"cool\" and \"wow\" factors of bringing the Fantastic Four and Avengers together for the first time was certainly the book's main attraction (as any glance at the cover could have told you), and don't get me wrong:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=seeing the teams together <i>was</i> somewhat interesting.]\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=But to be frank... <b>it wasn't <i>enough</i></b>.]\n\nNot only were there too many characters running around to make this adventure compelling enough to become a really memorable tale, Stan and Jack also resorted to their favorite [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=Have Hulk, Will Plot] catalyst for the haphazard team-up that only seemed to end when the page count had run its course.\n\nThis was never on display more blatantly than when Rick Jones whipped out his handy \"gamma-ray treated capsule\" and tossed it into the Hulk's mouth, allowing Stan and Jack to abruptly end the conflict in fewer panels than humans have fingers on one hand (even the <b><i>Thing's</i></b> hand, I might add).\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=large; text=<b>What the {</b><i>expletive-of-your-choice</i><b>}...??</b>]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(I challenge you to find one issue of the old <i>Hulk</i> magazine where such a capsule was <i>ever</i> mentioned!)]\n\n[HANG0] And the issue then <b>ends</b> with obligatory hand-shaking all around between the FF and Avengers.\n[BR] \"<i>Golly gee whiz</i>... it was sure glad to meet y'all -- be seeing you around, I guess!\"\n\nIt was a clear case of relying on spectacle to make up for [SPAN: severity=error; text=lazy plotting...] and the hope that everyone would be so caught up in the fact that the two teams were together, that no one would notice!&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Fat chance...)]\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=<b>To be fair</b>:&nbsp; the book wasn't <i>all </i>bad.]\n\nSome of the Thing's quips were [SPAN: severity=success; text=downright excellent], and I especially liked how he wanted the TV cameras to focus on him so that viewers could witness him in battle against the Hulk... and how mad he later became when he was covered by the wet cement, meaning that no one would be able to <i>recognize</i> him.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Oh, the <i>humanity</i>!)]\n\nHis comments to Thor were also laugh-out-loud funny:\n[HANG] * \"Why don'tcha learn how to <i>throw</i> that nutty gizmo!??!\"\n[HANG] * \"Say, Curly -- is that getup of yours for <i>real</i>??\"\n\nBut these best parts were things that Stan inserted into the dialogue that I wonder if Jack had any foreknowledge of.\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(I suspect not.)]\n\nIn the end, I <i>acknowledge</i> the effort here, and that the fact that it was <i>necessary</i> to have the two groups finally meet -- <i>expected even</i>.&nbsp; Yet it still does not compensate for the fact that it seems to have been done largely for the spectacle.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=The characterization that we witnessed last month was oddly absent this time around.]\n\nOutside of a handful of whimsical Thing commentary, the issue offered surprisingly little else.\n[BR] And it ultimately ended right back where last issue started.\n[HANG] * The Hulk is out of the picture (with no clue as to where he'll appear next)\n[HANG] * Reed is miraculously recovered\n[HANG] * The FF are back together\n[HANG] * Rick is staying with Captain America and the Avengers\n[HANG] * etc.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Congratulations, Stan and Jack:]\n[HANG] [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=With no genuine payoff of lasting consequence,]\n[HANG] [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=the almighty <b>RESET</b> button has been pushed.]",
  "side_note": [
    "[ASSET: left | CondemnedNeighborhood | width=12%] I know that the final battle occurred on a new construction site, but otherwise, the continued availability of \"deserted\" and \"abandoned\" and even \"condemned\" structures (and in this issue, a whole <i>neighborhood</i>) in the middle of NYC continues to shock me.\n[BR]\n[BR] (I wrote about this in my review of <i>last</i> month's issue as well.)"
  ],
  "letters_page": "The response to the letter by Reed J. Keonte was interesting in that it posed a contest where the readers write in to say what they think \"The Marvel Age of Comics\" actually means and states [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=italic; text=As usual, we'll award <b>no prizes</b>!&nbsp; If there are no winners, then no one can LOSE!]\n\nAs someone who's actually <i>seen</i> an official No-Prize that was owned by a college friend of mine (circa 1980), this makes me wonder when the practice started.&nbsp; I'll definitely keep my eyes peeled for the first official no-prize award!&nbsp; (In the meantime, the results of this contest was provided in the letters page of FF #31.)\n\nI think Bruce Solof's letter nicely summarizes what Marvel Comics meant to people of the day.&nbsp; Here it is, in its entirety:\n\n[HANG0] <i>Dear Stan and Jack,</i>\n[BR]<i> I am glad to see all these Marvel Comics.&nbsp; I'm starting to outgrow other companies' super-heroes but I don't want to give up comics.&nbsp; Now I don't have to because MARVEL is more adult.</i>\n\n<i>Wow</i>, that's a genuine testimonial if I've ever read one!",
  "decoder_ring": "<b>Retro-Reviewer's Note:</b>\n[BR] I acknowledge that, from my perspective as one who began his comics journey in the middle of the Bronze Age (1977), that team-ups between Marvel characters had become commonplace by then, and likely because of that, this issue didn't impress me as much as it might have to the readers plunking down their 12 cents in 1964.&nbsp; This issue may well have been a <i>massive</i> deal at that time, and perhaps I'm just not really appreciating it the way that contemporary readers would have.\n\n<b>It's possible this could well be the case.</b>  [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Trying to stay open-minded here...)]\n\nEven the \"Next Issue\" blurb about the upcoming team-up with Dr. Strange might well have been <i>enough</i> to excite the readers of the day.&nbsp; I'll try to keep this in mind as I continue this project.",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}